## Context

The observer is a small local web server the user keeps open beside their agent sessions: one per user per machine, showing every project. Four facts shape it. First, it runs alongside the pipeline but is never part of it: the recording side keeps each session's log, and the observer only reads those logs, receives copies of new events and shows them. Second, a server bound to the local machine is still reachable from any web page the user visits, and a domain name can be pointed at the local address to look same-origin, so "localhost only" is no boundary by itself. Third, it can start at any moment (late, after a crash, or because a newer AIDE took over) while events keep arriving from several sessions in several projects; the order in which it learns past and new events decides whether each shows exactly once. Fourth, sessions from different projects, possibly on different AIDE versions, share the one server, and each project's files must stay visible only inside that project's own view.

The address must be findable. Every session hands it out and open tabs hold it, yet the usual port may be taken by another program, so where the observer lands can vary between starts; a second server beside the first, or a link built from a guessed port, leaves the user watching a page that is not live. Claude Code's hooks, meanwhile, report presence apart from any session's server, and the page must show those changes as they happen.

## Strategy

**A short port list, the port taken recorded.** A start tries the user's port if set, then a short fixed list in order, and binds the first free one; the per-user record names the port taken, and every tool, link and hook ping reads it from there rather than assuming the first. A running observer never moves, and a replacement reuses its predecessor's port when free so open tabs keep working. An exhausted list fails the start, naming each port.

**Check the one per-user record before binding.** A start first reads the per-user record of the running observer and probes it with its token; if it answers, nothing new starts and the caller gets the link to its own session's view. The record is written only after the bind succeeds and removed on shutdown only if it still names this server, so a failed start neither strands a stale address nor erases a live one.

**Newer replaces older; older never replaces newer.** The record carries the running version. A newer caller sends a token-protected shutdown request, waits for that process to exit, then starts on its port when free; a same or newer server is kept. One way only, so two versions never swap the server back and forth; an old server that will not exit is named, never killed. Every event is on disk, so the replay loses nothing. The shutdown is a tool handshake, never a page control.

**Open the door before reading history, then merge.** The observer accepts posts and publishes its record before replaying every project's session logs, holding posts until the replay ends, then merges both keyed on session and order. An event recorded mid-replay sits in a log the replay reads, arrives as a post, or both; the merge keeps one copy, in order. Publishing after the replay leaves a gap: an event lands in a log already read and is never posted.

**Every request proves it came from this user's machine.** The observer listens on the loopback address only and refuses any request whose host is not that address and port, defeating a domain pointed at the local address. A post, presence ping or shutdown request must also carry the token kept in the user's own app data, and a post must be JSON; a cross-site page can do neither without a preflight the observer never grants.

**Home lists projects; a session view stays inside its project.** The home view lists every project with recorded sessions, live and past. A session's view is built from the root its project's newest session reported, since the shared server has no project of its own; its board is the kanban folder that project's brain config declares, under the kanban tag that session reported, and a terminal kanban-folder override applies only to the project it was run from. Every file read resolves inside that root and is refused outside it, however written.

**Reads rebuild; one request changes anything.** Each view is recomputed on every event, presence ping and page load from its project's files, every kept session's events, archived included, each session's liveness as the recording side judges it, and the board, never by watching files. A ping carries no state; the observer rereads presence from disk. Posts are validated and held in memory; the recording side stays the only writer. The one write deletes an ended session, refused otherwise.

## Good examples

- The user has a session open in one project with the observer running, then boots a second project. Its start finds the running observer, starts nothing, and returns the link to the new session's view; the home view now lists both projects, each opening onto its own tree and board.
- A session on a newer AIDE boots while an older observer runs. It asks the old one to shut down, waits for it to exit, and starts on the same port; the replay reads every project's logs, including two events posted mid-replay, and each shows once. When the older session next calls start, it finds the newer server and keeps it.
- In one project's session view, a request asks for a file through a path that climbs into a neighboring project with parent-directory steps. It is refused; the view keeps serving.

## Bad examples

- The first port is held by another dev server, so the observer takes the next on the list, but the start tool builds its link from the first port instead of the record, and the user's link opens someone else's app.
- Any version difference triggers a replacement, so an older session booting after a newer one shuts the newer server down. The two sessions swap the server on every start, and each swap drops every open tab's live stream.
- A session view resolves requested files against the folder of the session that started the server instead of its own project's reported root. The second project's view shows the first project's plan as its own.

